  <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-slate-900">Tibetan Barley Wine (Qingke Jiu)</h1>
  <p class="mt-3 text-lg leading-relaxed text-slate-500">The welcome drink of Tibet. Mild, slightly sour, served warm in a clay pot. Lower alcohol than grape wine, and inseparable from Tibetan hospitality. Offered to every guest who crosses a Tibetan threshold.</p>

  <section class="mt-12">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-slate-900">What Is Tibetan Barley Wine?</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-600">Tibetan barley wine (u9752u7a1eu9152, qingke jiu) is a traditional fermented alcoholic drink made from <strong>highland barley</strong> (qingke), the same grain used for tsampa. The barley is cooked, mixed with a traditional fermentation starter (jiuqu) containing wild yeasts and molds, and left to ferment for 3&ndash;7 days in clay jars. The resulting liquid is <strong>milky white to pale yellow, slightly effervescent, with 3&ndash;5% alcohol</strong> &mdash; comparable to a light unfiltered sake or Korean makgeolli.</p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-600">The flavor is mild and subtly sweet with a gentle sourness, often described as tasting like fermented rice water with a barley finish. It is always served <strong>warm</strong> in a small clay or metal pot, sometimes sweetened slightly for visitors. The warmth releases a toasty, cereal aroma that pairs beautifully with the cold mountain air of Shangri-La.</p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-600">In Tibetan culture, barley wine is the <strong>universal symbol of welcome and friendship</strong>. When you enter a Tibetan home, the host presents a cup of barley wine. Custom dictates you dip your ring finger into the wine, flick a droplet to the sky, to the earth, and to the center &mdash; an offering to the Buddha, the Dharma, and the Sangha &mdash; before drinking. The host refills your cup immediately; leaving it full signals you are done.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-12">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-slate-900">Where to Try Barley Wine in Shangri-La</h2>
    <div class="mt-4 space-y-5">
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-5">
        <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-slate-900">Tibetan Restaurants in Dukezong Old Town</h3>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600">Almost every Tibetan restaurant in the old town serves house-made barley wine. A pot (about 300ml) costs <strong>15&ndash;30 RMB</strong>. Many restaurants will offer a complimentary small cup as a welcome gesture. The most authentic experience is in family-run places with Tibetan-speaking owners.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-5">
        <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-slate-900">Homestays &amp; Farm Visits</h3>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600">The best barley wine is homemade. If you arrange a Tibetan village homestay or farm visit (available through guesthouses), you'll almost certainly be offered the family's own brew. This is the most authentic experience and often comes with the full welcoming ritual.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-5">
        <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-slate-900">Festivals</h3>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600">During the Horse Racing Festival (June, Dragon Boat Festival period) and Tibetan New Year (Losar, January/February), barley wine flows freely in the streets and public squares of Shangri-La.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-12">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-slate-900">Where to Buy Barley Wine</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-600"><strong>Bottled barley wine:</strong> Available in supermarkets and liquor stores in Shangri-La new town. <strong>20&ndash;50 RMB per 500ml bottle</strong>. Brands like Shangri-La Qingke Jiu are the most common. The bottled version is filtered and pasteurized &mdash; cleaner tasting but less characterful than the homemade unfiltered version.</p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-600"><strong>Handmade barley wine (gift packages):</strong> Sold in clay pots at craft shops in Dukezong Old Town. <strong>60&ndash;150 RMB per pot</strong> (300&ndash;500ml). These make beautiful souvenirs &mdash; the clay pot is as much a gift as the wine inside. Note that homemade barley wine has a short shelf life (1&ndash;2 weeks) and should be consumed soon after purchase.</p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-600"><strong>Barley wine at the morning market:</strong> Local farmers sell their own brew in recycled plastic bottles for <strong>10&ndash;20 RMB per 500ml</strong>. The most authentic and cheapest option, but the least predictable in quality and alcohol content.</p>
  </section>

